  "analysis": "We need to implement a solver for Codeforces 1538E - Funny Substrings. The program processes t test cases; each test case has n statements using either x := s (assign a literal string) or x = a + b (concatenate two previously defined strings). The final answer per test is the number of occurrences of the substring \"haha\" in the value of the last variable defined. Full strings can grow exponentially, so we must avoid storing them. We can maintain for every variable: the total count of occurrences within its value, the first up to 3 characters (pref), the last up to 3 characters (suff), and the total length (len). When concatenating A and B, the new count is cntA + cntB plus the number of cross-boundary occurrences of \"haha\" that straddle A and B. These cross occurrences can only involve up to 3 chars from A's end and up to 3 chars from B's start, so we test for k = 1..3 whether last k of A + first (4-k) of B equals \"haha\". The new pref is (prefA + prefB) truncated to 3; new suff is (suffA + suffB) taking last 3. Length is capped to avoid overflow (e.g., 1e18). The solution handles all edge cases and fits within limits. I will implement this in C++17, read input, maintain a map from variable names to Node structures, and print the cnt of the last-assigned variable for each test case. Then I’ll compile and run with the sample input to verify correctness.\n",
